# agent-svc runtime directories. /var/lib/agent-svc itself is created by the unit's
# StateDirectory= (0750 agent-svc:agent-svc); runs/ and publish/ under it are agent-svc's
# own state, created by the application (publish/ additionally gets 1-day cleanup below).
#
# Mirrors and the work root live under /srv/agent-svc so agent-codex (via the sudo'd
# codex_child.py) can read/clone them: agent-codex is a member of group agentwork, not
# of agent-svc's own primary group. Neither parent line carries an age: mirrors are
# never swept, and each run directory's own lifecycle is what should expire, not the
# work root itself.
d /srv/agent-svc 0750 agent-svc agentwork -
d /srv/agent-svc/mirrors 2750 agent-svc agentwork -
d /srv/agent-svc/work 2750 agent-svc agentwork -

# Each /srv/agent-svc/work/<run_id> is created by agent-svc itself (explicit chmod 3770:
# setgid so agent-codex's group-owned writes stay group agentwork, plus the sticky bit
# so one run's files can't be renamed/deleted by another run in the same group) when a
# run starts; this "e" line only ages out entries already below the work root
# (never recreates or re-chmods the root, thanks to the "-" placeholders) so a run
# directory left behind by a crash is swept after 3 days.
e /srv/agent-svc/work - - - 3d

d /var/lib/agent-svc/publish 0700 agent-svc agent-svc 1d

# agent "ops requests" (agent-svc-notes/ops-requests-spec.md, WP-D): state for the
# root oneshot unit ops/agent-ops-apply.service, which is its own separate process
# tree (not a child of agent-svc.service -- see that unit file), so its directories
# are root-owned, not agent-svc-owned, even though they live under a name that
# pairs with /var/lib/agent-svc. `results/` is 0750 root:agent-svc so agent-svc can
# READ (never write) the one result file it is waiting on; `backups/` and
# `docker-config/` hold real, unredacted env values (the one place that is
# unavoidable -- see env_apply.py's module docstring) and a docker CLI config
# directory respectively, so both stay 0700 root:root, unreadable by agent-svc or
# anything else. None of these are ever swept by age: every result/backup is a
# permanent audit trail until an operator removes it by hand.
d /var/lib/agent-ops 0750 root agent-svc -
d /var/lib/agent-ops/results 0750 root agent-svc -
d /var/lib/agent-ops/backups 0700 root root -
d /var/lib/agent-ops/docker-config 0700 root root -
d /var/log/agent-ops 0700 root root -

# Deliberately NO line here for /run/agent-svc/ops (where agent-svc writes
# request.json for env_apply.py to read): agent-svc.service declares
# `RuntimeDirectory=agent-svc`, so systemd itself owns /run/agent-svc's entire
# lifecycle -- created fresh (0755 agent-svc:agent-svc) on every start of
# agent-svc.service and REMOVED on every stop, including any subdirectory that
# was under it. A tmpfiles "d" line for /run/agent-svc/ops would only apply at
# boot (or a manual `systemd-tmpfiles --create`), not on every agent-svc restart,
# so it cannot be relied on to exist after a later `systemctl restart agent-svc`.
# agent-svc's own code must create /run/agent-svc/ops itself (0750
# agent-svc:agent-svc, e.g. via `os.makedirs(..., exist_ok=True)` followed by an
# explicit `os.chmod` since the directory-creation mode is subject to umask) --
# either once at startup or lazily right before it first writes request.json.
